{
  "run_id": "what_is_distributed_systems_basics_and_when_shou_8ce5141b",
  "task": "What is distributed systems basics and when should you use it?",
  "responses": [
    {
      "provider": "mistral:latest",
      "model": "mistral:latest",
      "source": "ollama",
      "text": "Distributed systems are a type of system composed of multiple autonomous computers that communicate and coordinate to achieve a common goal. They are designed to improve system reliability, scalability, and performance by distributing tasks, resources, and data across multiple nodes.\n\nHere are some basic concepts of distributed systems:\n\n1. Decentralization: Decision-making and control are distributed among multiple nodes, rather than being centralized in a single node.\n2. Autonomy: Each node in the system operates independently, making its own decisions and performing its own tasks.\n3. Communication: Nodes in a distributed system communicate with each other to coordinate their actions and share data.\n4. Fault tolerance: Distributed systems are designed to continue functioning even when some of the nodes fail.\n5. Consistency: Distributed systems must maintain consistency across all nodes to ensure that all data and operations are up-to-date and accurate.\n\nYou should use distributed systems when you need to build large-scale, highly available, and highly scalable systems that can handle a large number of requests and users. Examples of such systems include cloud computing platforms, social networks, e-commerce websites, and financial systems. Distributed systems are also useful when you need to build systems that can tolerate failures and continue functioning even when some of the nodes fail.",
      "error": ""
    }
  ]
}

